export const debate = {"id":"oconnor-turek-evil-moral-argument-2017","number":"27","assessmentModel":"5.6 Sol","assessmentRubric":"Slugfester Reassessment Rubric v2","title":"Alex O'Connor vs Frank Turek: The Moral Argument Debate","label":"Evil and moral objectivity","date":"2026-08-21","duration":"58 min","youtubeUrl":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=b5a3MxIqZOs","motion":"Does evil and objective morality point to God, or can agnostic atheism explain moral experience without accepting a divine moral ground?","sourceNote":"Based on the complete debate transcript. Summaries are condensed for clarity, and quotations are kept brief and faithful to the source.","scoringNote":"Scores are AI-generated estimates of argumentative performance and are intended as transparent, revisable judgments rather than final verdicts.","summary":"Turek grounds objective obligation in God; O'Connor distinguishes compelling moral experience from objectivity and offers evolutionary, psychological, and conditional explanations.","quotes":{"pro":{"text":"that's a difference between again epistemology and ontology","context":"Turek distinguishes uncertainty or disagreement about moral facts from the separate question whether an objective moral reality exists."},"con":{"text":"why people experience things as right or wrong based on science","context":"O'Connor limits evolutionary science to explaining moral experience, expressly withholding any inference from causal origin to genuine moral truth."}},"sides":{"pro":{"name":"Moral argument","speaker":"Frank Turek","color":"teal"},"con":{"name":"Subjective morality","speaker":"Alex O'Connor","color":"coral"}},"score":{"pro":76,"con":87},"sections":[{"sectionId":"section-burden-and-argument-scope","title":"Burden of Proof and Scope of the Moral Argument","timebox":"10:11–20:43","score":{"pro":86,"con":77},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-atheist-positive-claims-burden","time":"12:11","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Bare nonbelief may carry no burden, but atheists offering evolution, materialism, multiple universes, or quantum vacuums make positive explanatory claims requiring defense.","score":84,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply charitably accepts bare nonbelief while precisely isolating additional naturalistic explanations as affirmative commitments, thereby contacting both decisive components of the opponent's burden distinction without misrepresenting its qualification. Principal limitation: Its generalized description of what atheists ordinarily assert is not established in the excerpt, and the listed cosmological or metaphysical theories are not all shown to be commitments actually adopted by this interlocutor. Live burden: The challenge must connect each relevant positive explanation to O'Connor's expressed case and specify the evidence owed, rather than allowing observations about broader atheist discourse to substitute for engagement with his particular position. Locked score: The strong band reflects excellent responsiveness, logical structure, calibration, and representational charity, with narrower evidential and scope limitations preventing the reply from becoming independently decisive.","tags":[{"label":"Scope neglect","type":"bias","url":"https://cogbias.site/biases/scope-neglect/","context":"The generalization about atheists ranges beyond the narrower class of nonbelievers who actually affirm the listed explanatory claims."}]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-burden-agnostic-atheism-nonbelief","time":"10:11","role":"Supporting constructive","words":"Agnostic atheism means not knowing and therefore not believing; only someone positively asserting that no God exists assumes a corresponding evidential burden.","score":77,"critique":"Strongest feature: The distinction among uncertainty, nonbelief, and positive denial is explicit, terminologically stable, and carefully qualified, accurately limiting the burden principle to propositions actually affirmed by the speaker. Principal limitation: As a response, the clarification leaves untouched the preceding argument that evil presupposes goodness and that goodness has a divine ground, so its dialectical contribution is narrower than its conceptual clarity suggests. Live burden: The position must still address any affirmative naturalistic explanations it later adopts and explain why the proponent's positive grounding inference remains unsupported rather than relying exclusively on definitional burden allocation. Locked score: The strong band reflects coherent, calibrated burden analysis and adequate evidential support, moderated by limited contact with the earlier decisive inference and correspondingly restricted relevance to the central moral-grounding route.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-moral-argument-limited-conclusion","time":"20:25","role":"Concession or qualification","words":"The moral argument reaches only a divine ground for morality, not specifically Christianity; identifying the Christian God requires additional arguments within a cumulative case.","score":89,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply expressly concedes that another deity could satisfy the immediate conclusion, sharply distinguishing a generic divine foundation for morality from the identification of Christianity. Principal limitation: This disciplined qualification prevents overreach but independently provides neither evidence for a divine foundation nor substantiation that supplementary arguments complete the broader religious identification. Live burden: The pro case must establish the narrower grounding inference on its own merits, without allowing references to Christianity to import evidential support unavailable from the moral argument itself. Locked score: The very strong band reflects a direct and complete answer, terminological stability, explicit qualification, proportionate possibility language, and unusually disciplined conclusion management, limited only by the inherent evidential modesty of scope clarification.","tags":[]},"con":null}]},{"sectionId":"section-objective-moral-grounding","title":"Objective Moral Obligation and Divine Grounding","timebox":"7:17–38:21","score":{"pro":78,"con":89},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-evil-good-divine-ground","time":"7:17","role":"Load-bearing constructive","words":"Evil exists parasitically as a lack within good; because goodness is God's nature, recognizing evil presupposes an ultimate divine standard of goodness.","score":74,"critique":"Strongest feature: The privation analogy intelligibly explains evil as corruption rather than an independently existing substance, directly connecting experienced wrongdoing with the debate's central demand for an ultimate standard. Principal limitation: The reasoning identifies goodness with God's nature without independently establishing that identity, so the illustrative rust comparison cannot substantiate the decisive metaphysical transition from goodness generally to divine goodness. Live burden: The argument must distinguish conceptual dependence on good from ontological dependence on God and demonstrate why secular moral realism, natural properties, or irreducible normative facts cannot satisfy that explanatory requirement. Locked score: The mixed band appropriately recognizes exceptional motion relevance and a recoverable constructive structure while registering materially overstated necessity, unstable terminology, absent qualification, and insufficient independent warrant for the divine-grounding premise.","tags":[{"label":"Begging the question","type":"fallacy","url":"https://logfall.com/fallacies/begging-the-question/","context":"The decisive identification of goodness with God's nature is assumed inside the inference intended to establish a divine moral ground."}]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-objective-morality-evidential-concession","time":"20:59","role":"Major direct reply","words":"If objective morality were demonstrated, naturalism would need further explanation; showing that conduct produces prosperity does not prove prosperity itself morally good.","score":91,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply combines a meaningful evidential concession with a precise diagnosis of the gap between objectively measurable social consequences and the unproved evaluative premise that prosperity is morally good. Principal limitation: Criticizing one scientific proposal does not establish subjectivism, exhaust naturalistic moral realism, or demonstrate that every nondriven account of objective normativity contains the same assumption. Live burden: The con case must preserve this causal-normative distinction while explaining its own moral vocabulary and clarifying whether it merely suspends judgment about objectivity or positively locates value within attitudes. Locked score: The very strong band reflects exceptional calibration, stable scope, direct responsiveness, and a well-supported assumption-gap objection, while appropriately stopping short of treating the failure of a particular proposal as comprehensive evidence against objective morality.","tags":[{"label":"Begging the question","type":"fallacy","url":"https://logfall.com/fallacies/begging-the-question/","context":"The criticized scientific proposal presupposes that prosperous societies are morally good while presenting consequences for prosperity as proof of objective morality."}]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-basic-objective-obligation","time":"37:29","role":"Supporting constructive","words":"Immediate awareness of categorical moral obligation can function as basic evidence because it appears more obvious than the skeptical premises offered against objective morality.","score":82,"critique":"Strongest feature: The move presents immediate moral obligation as prima facie evidence rather than attempting an impossible derivation from neutral premises, and it frames the issue through a clear comparative-obviousness argument. Principal limitation: The passage shifts among objective value, objective obligation, and a powerful subjective sense of obligation while relying on a quoted philosopher's assessment without independently comparing reliability, defeaters, or alternative explanations. Live burden: The pro case must articulate conditions for properly basic moral belief, explain its defeasibility, address conflicting intuitions and evolutionary influence, and establish why phenomenological force tracks mind-independent truth. Locked score: The strong band reflects substantial relevance, a coherent evidential strategy, and a direct response to skepticism, moderated by partly unstable scope, implicit qualification, slight overstatement, and incomplete assessment of competing skeptical premises.","tags":[{"label":"Appeal to authority","type":"fallacy","url":"https://logfall.com/fallacies/appeal-to-authority/","context":"The quoted philosopher's status and personal assessment carry argumentative weight without the underlying reliability comparison being independently developed."}]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-conscious-moral-status-circular-grounding","time":"23:15","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Wrongness depends on conscious psychology; invoking a transcendent mind to secure objectivity before using objective morality to prove God risks circular grounding.","score":87,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply identifies circularity in using a transcendent being to guarantee moral objectivity and then treating that objectivity as evidence for the being, clarifying the objection. Principal limitation: The objection presumes neither independent reasons for divine reality nor a noninferential account of God's grounding role, so its formulation exceeds the excerpt's support. Live burden: The con case must demonstrate that consciousness dependence entails attitude dependence, while answering moral arguments treating God as independently supported rather than introduced exclusively for this inference. Locked score: The very strong band reflects precise contact, coherence, explicit qualification, and direct engagement with indispensable divine grounding, tempered by overstatement about circularity and an incompletely defended transition from psychological dependence to moral subjectivism.","tags":[]}}]},{"sectionId":"section-evolutionary-moral-experience","title":"Evolutionary and Psychological Explanation of Moral Experience","timebox":"25:03–47:23","score":{"pro":73,"con":91},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-evolution-global-belief-defeater","time":"26:45","role":"Major direct reply","words":"If evolution explains every thought, it threatens trust in every belief; obvious moral knowledge should not be dismissed merely to preserve an evolutionary account.","score":73,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply identifies an important self-referential reliability question for any evolutionary explanation that purports to encompass cognition generally, and it states the alleged defeating consequence with unmistakable dialectical directness. Principal limitation: Evolutionary causation is treated as automatically hostile to truth without considering selection for reliably successful representation, cultural correction, domain-specific error, or independent methods for evaluating cognitive performance. Live burden: The pro case must establish a principled connection between fitness-directed origins and epistemic unreliability, distinguish moral cognition from cognition generally, and avoid treating the vivid certainty of a moral judgment as validation of its objective status. Locked score: The mixed band recognizes diagnostic responsiveness and central relevance while reflecting materially overstated necessity, unstable scope, missing qualification, and inadequate support for the proposed global defeater.","tags":[{"label":"Scope neglect","type":"bias","url":"https://cogbias.site/biases/scope-neglect/","context":"The argument expands from evolutionary influence on moral cognition to the unreliability of every belief without establishing the global scope."}]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-evolved-dispositions-conditional-rules","time":"25:03","role":"Supporting constructive","words":"Evolution supplies survival-oriented dispositions from which conditionally objective behavioral rules follow once well-being is adopted, although consensus cannot create mind-independent morality.","score":86,"critique":"Strongest feature: The move offers a concrete evolutionary explanation for shared dispositions while limiting behavioral rules to survival or well-being objectives and denying that unanimity establishes mind independence. Principal limitation: Calling goal-relative conclusions objective destabilizes the central terminology, because conditionally valid guidance remains different from categorical moral truth and objectivity independent of valuing consciousness. Live burden: The account must explain why ends possess authority, how conflicts among survival, flourishing, and preference are resolved, and whether conditional prescriptions merit specifically moral rather than merely instrumental standing. Locked score: The very strong band recognizes explanatory possibility, substantial motion contact, and explicit qualifications, while terminological instability and incomplete normative grounding prevent the evolutionary account from resolving the ontological dispute.","tags":[{"label":"Equivocation","type":"fallacy","url":"https://logfall.com/fallacies/equivocation/","context":"The description moves between goal-relative correctness and mind-independent moral objectivity under the same term."}]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-evolution-self-reference-basic-belief","time":"46:54","role":"Major direct reply","words":"An evolutionary account allegedly cannot certify itself if every thought is evolution-produced, whereas moral conviction may qualify as properly basic rather than merely assumed.","score":72,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply identifies a genuine self-referential problem for comprehensive evolutionary debunking and contrasts an unavoidable assumption with the epistemologically richer category of properly basic belief. Principal limitation: It expands the opponent’s causal explanation into claiming every thought is evolution-produced, presumes causation defeats justification, and invokes proper basicality without specifying reliability, defeasibility, or warrant conditions. Live burden: Pro must establish why evolved mechanisms cannot track truth, explain why correction cannot escape the defeater, and show why moral conviction qualifies as basic while evolutionary belief receives no comparable entitlement. Locked score: The mixed band reflects diagnostic relevance and recoverable challenge but recognizes overstated necessity, unstable scope and terminology, missing qualification, and inadequate support for self-defeat claim and epistemic asymmetry.","tags":[{"label":"Special pleading","type":"fallacy","url":"https://logfall.com/fallacies/special-pleading/","context":"Moral conviction receives the label properly basic while the evolutionary account is denied comparable entitlement without a stated principled asymmetry."}]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-causal-explanation-not-moral-justification","time":"38:56","role":"Load-bearing constructive","words":"Science can causally explain why people experience conduct as right or wrong without proving that the conduct really possesses either moral property.","score":95,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply states with exceptional precision that explaining the causal origin of moral experience is logically distinct from establishing the truth of its content, directly defeating an explanatory-to-normative inference. Principal limitation: This narrow distinction leaves unresolved whether any additional naturalistic premises could justify moral conclusions and does not itself establish that moral truth is subjective, nonexistent, or dependent on conscious attitudes. Live burden: The con case must retain this disciplined limitation while explaining what practical authority its evolved attitudes possess and whether the resulting view is error theory, constructivism, relativism, or another metaethical position. Locked score: The exceptional band reflects complete proposition recovery, stable terminology, exact scope, explicit qualification, matched categorical force, decisive responsiveness, and an unusually well-supported inference, without inflating its deliberately limited conclusion.","tags":[]}}]},{"sectionId":"section-reason-logic-and-reliability","title":"Reason, Logic, and Evolutionary Reliability","timebox":"29:00–32:47","score":{"pro":75,"con":83},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-logic-reason-naturalist-grounding","time":"29:21","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Assuming evolved reason does not explain its trustworthiness, while objective and immaterial logical laws remain unexplained if natural reality consists only of material objects.","score":75,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply distinguishes presupposing reason from explaining its trustworthiness and raises an ontological question about logical laws, contacting epistemic reliability and naturalistic grounding with interrogative force. Principal limitation: Calling logical laws nonmolecular does not establish their need for supernatural ontological explanation, while leaving unspecified which version of naturalism confronts the objection. Live burden: Clarify whether logical laws are propositions, relations, abstractions, or norms, demonstrate why nominalist, conceptualist, structural, or realist naturalistic treatments are inadequate, and explain why divine cognition provides a superior account. Locked score: The strong band recognizes excellent responsiveness, relevance, and proportionate questioning, moderated by partly unstable terminology, implicit qualification, and no developed inference from nonmateriality to a divine ground.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-reason-necessary-epistemic-baseline","time":"29:00","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Reason cannot be certified without using reason, yet inquiry necessarily begins by assuming reason, existence, and an external world rather than proving them noncircularly.","score":85,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply candidly acknowledges that consensus cannot prove reason true and presents rational practice as an unavoidable shared starting point, avoiding a pretended noncircular validation that the position cannot provide. Principal limitation: Practical indispensability alone does not answer the deeper explanatory challenge about why evolved reasoning is generally reliable, nor does comparison with existence or an external world establish parity among these assumptions. Live burden: The account must distinguish entitlement to employ reason from a causal explanation of its reliability and show how error detection, prediction, and intersubjective correction provide warrant beyond unavoidable reliance. Locked score: The very strong band reflects a direct, stable, explicitly qualified, and appropriately calibrated answer to the justificatory demand, while leaving the proponent's separate ontological and evolutionary reliability questions only partially addressed.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-subjective-reason-self-defeat","time":"32:29","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Declaring reason subjective appears self-defeating because presenting that conclusion as true relies upon objective rational warrant and the possibility of genuine knowledge.","score":74,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply clearly articulates a self-reference test: a universal denial of objective rational warrant would seemingly rely on the very truth-directed reasoning it discredits, producing a forceful diagnostic challenge. Principal limitation: The opponent distinguished objective logical rules from assumption-dependent reasoning, so interpreting that position as denying every possibility of true conclusion exaggerates its scope and makes the alleged contradiction easier to obtain. Live burden: The pro case must address the opponent's qualified thesis, distinguish subjective premise choice from subjective validity, and establish self-defeat without substituting a stronger universal skepticism that was not actually asserted. Locked score: The mixed band appropriately credits clarity, relevance, and explicit diagnostic structure while registering materially overstated necessity, missing qualification, and insufficient representational charity toward the rule-versus-premise distinction under discussion.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-objective-logic-subjective-reasoning","time":"30:02","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Logical rules may be objective even when reasoning from adopted premises is assumption-dependent, paralleling conditionally valid moral conclusions drawn from chosen goals.","score":80,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply preserves objective logical constraints while distinguishing valid inferential relations from conclusions conditioned by adopted premises, offering a coherent compatibility answer instead of denying reasoning’s normative architecture. Principal limitation: Describing reason itself as subjective conflates discretionary premise selection with inferential validity, creating terminological instability and obscuring whether subjectivity concerns psychology, justification, or logical consequence. Live burden: Con must formulate this distinction, explain why objective validity requires no divine ontology, and demonstrate how premise-relative moral conclusions differ from categorical obligations without collapsing either category. Locked score: Strong band recognizes direct engagement, coherent structure, scope stability, and proportionate possibility language, while the ambiguous account of reason and incomplete ontology of norms prevent resolution of the grounding challenge.","tags":[{"label":"Equivocation","type":"fallacy","url":"https://logfall.com/fallacies/equivocation/","context":"The word subjective shifts between chosen premises and the validity or status of reasoning itself."}]}}]},{"sectionId":"section-disagreement-and-practical-judgment","title":"Moral Disagreement, Cultural Variation, and Practical Judgment","timebox":"35:38–50:04","score":{"pro":73,"con":90},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-better-society-implies-best","time":"40:42","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Calling one society better than another supposedly implies an objective best beyond preference, prompting the question of what ontologically grounds that ultimate standard.","score":63,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply recognizes that comparative moral language can conceal evaluative criteria and properly asks what makes social improvement more than the satisfaction of presently shared preferences. Principal limitation: It moves from better to a unique best, then from that superlative to an objective standard and finally to an external ontological ground, treating distinct comparative and metaphysical concepts as interchangeable without argument. Live burden: The pro case must exclude plural goods, incomplete orderings, local comparisons, and criterion-relative improvement before inferring an absolute optimum, then independently connect any objective standard to God. Locked score: The weak band reflects meaningful relevance and a recoverable challenge but appropriately records material terminological instability, missing qualification, overstated necessity, limited responsiveness to the causal-normative distinction, and inadequate warrant for each transition in the inference.","tags":[{"label":"Equivocation","type":"fallacy","url":"https://logfall.com/fallacies/equivocation/","context":"Better, best, objective standard, and ontological ground perform different argumentative functions but are treated as interchangeable."}]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-compelling-yet-culturally-contingent-judgment","time":"35:38","role":"Supporting constructive","words":"Racism's wrongness may feel as instinctively certain as elementary arithmetic while remaining culturally contingent, because psychological compulsion does not establish mind-independent truth.","score":89,"critique":"Strongest feature: The comparison carefully separates the psychological force of a judgment from its objective status, using historical variation to demonstrate that confidence and cultural convergence cannot independently settle metaethical reality. Principal limitation: Cultural contingency shows that moral intuitions vary and may be unreliable, but it neither proves subjectivism nor explains why some judgments improve through criticism, information, or widening consideration. Live burden: The con case must provide criteria for distinguishing distortion from justified revision, clarify whether variation is merely underdetermining evidence or positive evidence against objectivity, and preserve grounds for condemning entrenched injustice. Locked score: The very strong band reflects precise terminology, stable scope, explicit qualification, direct responsiveness, and excellent calibration, while appropriately recognizing that the example blocks one inference without establishing a complete subjective metaethics.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-disagreement-not-unreality","time":"49:29","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Moral disagreement concerns epistemic access rather than ontology; scientific disagreement about reality likewise does not imply that an objective world fails to exist.","score":89,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply precisely separates epistemological uncertainty from ontological nonexistence, deploying the scientific-disagreement analogy to decisively invalidate the inference that incomplete clarity or disagreement alone makes objective morality unreal. Principal limitation: This successful defensive demonstration neither establishes moral facts nor identifies dependable epistemic access, and it leaves unanswered whether disagreement’s extent and pattern supply probabilistic evidence against moral objectivity. Live burden: The pro case must provide affirmative grounding and explain how moral inquiry reliably discriminates authentic discovery from cultural projection, entrenched bias, evolutionary influence, and persistently conflicting intuition. Locked score: The very strong band recognizes exact scope, terminological stability, explicit qualification, decisive responsiveness, categorical-force alignment, and substantial charity, while restricting credit to defeating entailment rather than proving objectivity or divine grounding.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-revulsion-does-not-refute-subjectivism","time":"45:32","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Even if naturalism makes morality evolutionarily arbitrary, recoiling from that consequence does not show naturalism false or establish objective moral facts.","score":92,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply concedes the emotionally unwelcome implication and then cleanly separates revulsion from evidence, applying the same standard to naturalists offended by arbitrariness and atheists offended by depictions of God. Principal limitation: Demonstrating that recoil is not a refutation does not positively establish subjectivism or answer whether persistent moral experience supplies defeasible evidence beyond emotion alone. Live burden: The con case must explain why moral seeming lacks objective evidential force, articulate the practical consequences of evolutionary arbitrariness, and avoid allowing symmetrical emotional criticism to replace a substantive account of normativity. Locked score: The very strong band reflects decisive diagnosis, stable terminology, explicit limitation, matched categorical force, and strong representational balance, while its intentionally narrow conclusion leaves the broader metaphysical and justificatory questions open.","tags":[{"label":"Subjective validation","type":"bias","url":"https://cogbias.site/biases/subjective-validation/","context":"The rejected inference treats emotional recoil from a worldview's implication as evidence that the implication or worldview is false."}]}}]}],"overall":{"pro":{"score":76,"strengths":["Carefully limited the moral argument's conclusion to a generic divine moral ground rather than claiming that morality alone establishes Christianity.","Correctly distinguished disagreement about moral reality from the separate ontological question whether such a reality exists.","Charitably accepted that bare nonbelief carries no positive explanatory burden while assigning burdens to additional affirmative naturalistic claims.","Pressed substantive questions about evolutionary cognitive reliability, logical normativity, and the evidential standing of immediate moral obligation."],"blunders":[{"text":"The foundational move identified goodness with God's nature without independently defending that identity, leaving the divine-grounding bridge under-supported.","links":[{"label":"Begging the question","url":"https://logfall.com/fallacies/begging-the-question/"}]},{"text":"The evolutionary defeater repeatedly expanded causal influence into global unreliability without addressing truth-tracking, correction, or domain-specific reliability.","links":[{"label":"Scope neglect","url":"https://cogbias.site/biases/scope-neglect/"}]},{"text":"The claim that comparative improvement implies a unique best and then a divine ontological ground compressed several unsupported transitions.","links":[{"label":"Equivocation","url":"https://logfall.com/fallacies/equivocation/"}]}]},"con":{"score":87,"strengths":["Separated causal explanations of moral experience from justification of moral truth with exceptional clarity and disciplined scope.","Conceded the explanatory significance of demonstrated objective morality while exposing the assumed value premise in prosperity-based scientific accounts.","Explained how evolved dispositions can generate conditional practical rules without claiming that agreement creates mind-independent morality.","Distinguished psychological certainty from objectivity and showed why cultural variation undercuts any simple inference from compelling experience.","Consistently rejected emotional revulsion as a substitute for evidence, applying that principle symmetrically across worldviews."],"blunders":[{"text":"The burden clarification did not answer the earlier privation and divine-grounding inference, limiting its immediate responsiveness.","links":[]},{"text":"Calling reason subjective and conditional moral rules objective blurred premise dependence, logical validity, and mind-independent normativity.","links":[{"label":"Equivocation","url":"https://logfall.com/fallacies/equivocation/"}]},{"text":"The circularity objection to divine grounding assumed too quickly that the proponent possessed no independent route to God.","links":[]}]}},"logicalExtension":{"pro":{"finalArgument":{"thesis":"The AI steelman is that categorical moral obligation is best explained by a necessarily good personal ground, although this abductive conclusion neither follows from moral experience alone nor identifies a particular religion.","premises":["Some moral experiences present themselves not merely as preferences or useful instructions but as categorical reasons that apply despite an agent's contrary desires.","Evolutionary and cultural explanations of why such experiences arise are compatible with their truth, because a causal genealogy alone does not determine whether their content is warranted.","A complete account of categorical obligation must explain both normative authority and why persons, as deliberating agents, are appropriately addressed by that authority.","A necessarily good personal foundation may unify value, authority, and accountability more economically than accounts treating normative facts as causally and personally unexplained abstractions.","That explanatory advantage is defeasible and depends on showing that divine goodness is principled rather than arbitrary and that competing realist accounts explain less."],"conclusion":"Therefore, categorical moral experience provides qualified abductive support for a necessarily good personal ground if that hypothesis explains authority better than its alternatives, but it does not independently establish Christianity."},"newArguments":[{"title":"Second-personal authority","text":"Moral obligations characteristically address agents as claims that can demand justification, apology, and accountability, not merely as descriptions of optimal conduct. An impersonal moral fact might specify what is valuable, yet leave unexplained why that value authoritatively addresses a person. A necessarily good personal ground could connect value with reason-giving authority without inferring God merely from strong emotion. This argument remains comparative: it succeeds only if impersonal realism, constructivism, and social accounts cannot explain second-personal authority equally well."},{"title":"Evolution without global skepticism","text":"An evolutionary genealogy need not defeat every belief. The narrower challenge asks whether a disposition would have promoted fitness across many incompatible moral propositions and whether reflective checking independently discriminates among them. If a particular moral seeming remains stable under information, impartial perspective-taking, coherence testing, and exposure to counterexamples, its genealogy alone supplies no decisive defeater. This approach preserves the pro concern about reliability while abandoning the unsupported claim that evolutionary causation automatically discredits all cognition."}]},"con":{"finalArgument":{"thesis":"The AI steelman is that moral experience can be explained and practically disciplined through conscious interests, social coordination, and reflective procedures without establishing mind-independent value or a divine moral ground.","premises":["Evolution, learning, empathy, and social dependence plausibly explain why humans possess powerful dispositions concerning harm, reciprocity, loyalty, and cooperation.","The intensity, convergence, or involuntary character of a moral seeming does not by itself establish that its content is true independently of every valuing mind.","Causal explanations of moral experience and justificatory procedures for revising conduct answer different questions, so explaining origins need not pretend to prove objective moral properties.","Public standards can be constructed through informed agreement, consistency, reciprocity, and protection against domination, even when their authority remains dependent on conscious agents and shared practices.","Invoking God does not improve the explanation unless independent evidence supports that being and a noncircular account connects divine nature with reasons binding human agents."],"conclusion":"Therefore, moral experience does not presently require a divine or mind-independent ground; a conscious-agent and procedural account remains viable while leaving broader metaphysical possibilities open."},"newArguments":[{"title":"Procedural moral authority","text":"Subject dependence need not reduce morality to each individual's momentary preference. Agents can ask which rules informed persons could justify to one another under conditions protecting voice, consistency, reciprocity, and freedom from coercive domination. Standards produced through that procedure remain grounded in conscious interests, yet they can criticize existing consensus and personal desire. The account explains moral revision without positing stance-independent properties: reforms occur when excluded interests become visible, factual errors are corrected, or accepted principles are applied more consistently."},{"title":"Explanatory underdetermination","text":"Even if objective obligation were granted, the inference to God would remain underdetermined. Irreducible normative realism, naturalist realism, ideal-observer theories, and constructivist accounts offer different explanations of apparent authority. A divine hypothesis gains support only after comparative criteria are specified and those alternatives perform worse. Thus the con side need not prove that every divine ground is impossible; it can require the pro side to show why personality, necessity, and perfect goodness are explanatory consequences rather than attributes inserted to reproduce the moral data."}]}},"year":2017};
